Irregular Astigmatism (Less Common): The curves are uneven and not perpendicular, often caused by eye injuries, scarring, or conditions like keratoconus.
How Is It Diagnosed & Measured?
An eye care professional (optometrist or ophthalmologist) diagnoses astigmatism through a routine comprehensive eye exam using:
Visual Acuity Test: Reading the eye chart.
Phoropter & Retinoscope: Testing different lenses in front of your eyes to measure refraction.
Keratometer / Corneal Topography: Mapping the exact curvature and surface contour of your cornea.
On an eyeglass prescription, astigmatism is represented by two specific numbers:
CYL (Cylinder): Indicates the strength or severity of the astigmatism (measured in diopters).
